          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Two cylinders have equal volumes. The first has radius 6 cm and height 10 cm; the second has radius 3 cm. Determine the second cylinder\'s height, and explain why it is four times the first cylinder\'s height.',
          answer: 'The first cylinder\'s volume is π × 6² × 10 = 360π cm³. For the second, 360π = π × 3² × h = 9πh, so h = 40 cm. It is four times the first height because halving the radius from 6 to 3 QUARTERS the base area, from 36π to 9π — the radius is squared. With the volume fixed, the height must be four times as great to make up for a base one quarter the size.',
